WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:06.060
A sneaky Cyberpunk 2077 reference and a&nbsp;
seer with a prophecy to end the world of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:11.024
the Witcher. "The Witcher: Blood Origin"&nbsp;
hid some insane details in its storyline.

00:00:11.024 --> 00:00:16.260
A particularly joyous Easter egg comes in the&nbsp;
third episode of "The Witcher: Blood Origin," when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:21.120
the show's hodgepodge team of unlikely misfits&nbsp;
strategize over how to defeat an interdimensional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:25.601
monster. With the group all but accepting&nbsp;
their powerlessness, Brother Death offers,

00:00:25.601 --> 00:00:27.821
"Why don't we poison a sheep and feed it to it?"

00:00:27.821 --> 00:00:32.280
The moment is a funny one on its own, but it&nbsp;
becomes even funnier when you realize that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:37.020
the idea is a direct reference to both the&nbsp;
"Witcher" books and the main Netflix series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:39.660
which feature the very same sheep-based strategy.

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:44.400
In "The Bounds of Reason," a short story&nbsp;
in the book "Sword of Destiny," Geralt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.340
and a large band of adventurers&nbsp;
hunt for an alleged golden dragon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:51.180
that has been terrorizing a small&nbsp;
village. Before Geralt's arrival,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:56.040
a local named Sheepbagger had filled a&nbsp;
dead sheep with a number of poisons, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.040 --> 00:01:01.140
apparently worked and killed the dragon. Geralt&nbsp;
arrives as another dragon begins attacking anew,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:06.840
and once again, Sheepbagger offers a poisoned&nbsp;
sheep as a plan. The story is adapted for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:12.180
sixth episode of Season 1 of "The Witcher," this&nbsp;
time without Sheepbagger and his ovine offense.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:17.340
As "The Witcher: Blood Origin" begins, we're&nbsp;
treated to a surprise scene starring everyone's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:22.560
favorite lecherous bard, Jaskier, in the modern&nbsp;
era. Though he's saved from a chaotic scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:27.060
of blood and death, it's obvious that he isn't&nbsp;
out of the woods when his rescuer is revealed:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:32.400
the mysterious figure played by Minnie Driver. Her&nbsp;
rescue is as horrifying as the battle before it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:37.560
as is her inestimable amount of magic power, much&nbsp;
of which she shares with another mysterious figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:41.820
from the Witcher lore: Gaunter O'Dimm&nbsp;
from the video game "The Witcher III."

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:47.100
In the game, O'Dimm takes a special interest&nbsp;
in Geralt, which he never quite explains and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:52.020
results in the figure helping and harming the&nbsp;
Witcher's quest according to his whim. Likewise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:56.700
Driver's character takes a special interest&nbsp;
in Jaskier, which she also never explains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:02.100
aside from calling herself a fellow storyteller.&nbsp;
They both upend the lives of their chosen mortals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:06.300
and they display a striking amount of&nbsp;
similarities in their godlike powers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:10.260
dubious morality. Both display the&nbsp;
ability to freeze time completely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:15.240
and both wander freely among the frozen&nbsp;
populace, aiding or killing them at will.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:19.320
On the other hand, this mysterious&nbsp;
character might not be a reference to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:23.701
O'Dimm at all. After first appearing to&nbsp;
Jaskier in his own form, she tells him,

00:02:23.701 --> 00:02:25.921
"I have other faces ... if you prefer."

00:02:25.921 --> 00:02:29.460
The combination of that shape and that&nbsp;
mask strongly resembles the Crones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:33.840
or Ladies of the Wood, from "The Witcher&nbsp;
III," specifically the hulking Brewess.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:37.500
Brewess and her two sisters may have&nbsp;
been referenced on Netflix before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:42.240
in the form of the Cabin Witch from Season 2&nbsp;
of "The Witcher." All these characters share&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:46.320
a number of traits, many of which can be&nbsp;
traced back to the Slavic folklore figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:50.820
Baba Yaga. Whether or not this new character&nbsp;
is revealed to be one of the Crones or not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:55.438
she is certainly a part of the Netflix&nbsp;
series' habit of hinting at their existence.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:59.580
In "The Witcher: Blood Origin," there is&nbsp;
no character whose importance to the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:03.780
is more significant than it seems than&nbsp;
Avallac'h, the young Elvish apprentice.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:07.860
Fans of the books and video games alike&nbsp;
will recognize the name as one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:11.880
important in the history of Elvendom,&nbsp;
and specifically the history of Ciri.

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:17.100
In both the books and video games, Avallac'h&nbsp;
is an old Elf with an immense amount of magical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:21.600
knowledge and power. More so in the games,&nbsp;
he becomes Ciri's mentor and protector,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:26.280
teaching her how to safely wield her powers&nbsp;
and keeping them from falling into the wrong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:30.660
hands. Given the young Avallac'h's appearance&nbsp;
in the post-credits scene of the miniseries's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:35.700
last episode, he may yet fulfill some of the same&nbsp;
grand duties as his counterparts in other media.

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:41.460
Another small role in "The Witcher: Blood Origin"&nbsp;
is Ithlinne. The young girl appears only briefly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:46.080
in the first and last episodes, bookending&nbsp;
the miniseries with quick teases at her true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:50.880
nature. In both episodes, she enters trances&nbsp;
and delivers prophecies to Éile, The Lark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:56.160
demonstrating a natural magical affinity&nbsp;
and the rare gift of foresight. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:00.540
for fans of the books, games, and other&nbsp;
media, Ithlinne and her prophecies mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:03.900
so much more than mere guidance to&nbsp;
a single Elf warrior — they're the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:07.440
reason for all the machinations that&nbsp;
end up enveloping Ciri and Geralt.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:11.700
Though the Ithlinne we meet in the series is&nbsp;
just a young girl, she'll grow up to become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:16.320
the greatest seer of her age, and potentially&nbsp;
in the history of the Witcher timeline. She's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:20.460
known for a number of prophecies that come&nbsp;
true, but more than anything for one that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:24.501
has yet to come true. Simply known as&nbsp;
Ithlinne's Prophecy, it reads in part,

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:41.100
In the eyes of many of the Continent's wisest&nbsp;
and most powerful, that Elder Blood must be Ciri,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:46.099
meaning whoever controls Ciri, controls&nbsp;
the ending and rebirth of the world.

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:50.640
It's thanks to the old Elven mage Balor that&nbsp;
we hear one of the most subtle Easter eggs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:55.500
in all of "The Witcher: Blood Origin." The&nbsp;
mage is selfish, ruthless, power-hungry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.500 --> 00:05:00.480
and spiteful, which is why, after he banishes&nbsp;
political rival Eredin to another world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:05.700
he can't resist one final curse. As he&nbsp;
expels Eredin and his men, Balor adds,

00:05:05.734 --> 00:05:09.478
"Wherever this takes you, may&nbsp;
you burn for all eternity."

00:05:09.478 --> 00:05:13.739
For Witcher fans who know Eredin's&nbsp;
destiny, the line is ironic and cheeky.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.739 --> 00:05:18.300
The Eredin in the books, games, and other media&nbsp;
eventually becomes The King of the Wild Hunt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:22.860
a title given to him for not only commanding&nbsp;
the fearsome raiding party the Wild Hunt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:26.040
but for leading their attacks from&nbsp;
the front as an ancient king might.

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:31.860
The irony in Balor's last curse upon Eredin comes&nbsp;
in how the Wild Hunt operates: all their raids&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:37.020
are fought with frost magic. Specifically, they&nbsp;
use pieces of the all-consuming White Frost to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:42.060
inflict devastating premature winters on their&nbsp;
targets, turn them to ice using frost magic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:47.400
and even employ dog-like ice elementals&nbsp;
as their frontline offense. Not only is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:51.780
Eredin not burning for all eternity, he's&nbsp;
actually freezing more than almost anyone.

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:56.280
It's no secret to those who pay attention&nbsp;
that the elves of the Witcher world are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.280 --> 00:06:01.620
heavily inspired by Celtic mythology and&nbsp;
actual Irish history. Their place-names,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:06.480
surnames, and even the name of their species&nbsp;
are overwhelmingly Gaelic. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:11.880
the two main races of elves are known as the Aen&nbsp;
Seidhe — an obvious play on the aes sídhe, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:16.560
fairy folk in Celtic mythology, whose name&nbsp;
literally means "people of the mound" — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:20.760
the Aen Elle, also a play on the same,&nbsp;
this time meaning "people of the alders."

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:25.440
In "The Witcher: Blood Origin," the connection&nbsp;
between elves and Ireland is taken a step further&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:29.940
by the Lark's song, "The Black Rose." The bard&nbsp;
sings the ballad multiple times throughout the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:35.280
miniseries and ultimately inspires revolution&nbsp;
among the lower-class citizens. The title of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:40.020
the song is intentionally referential to Irish&nbsp;
culture: the Black Rose is a well-known symbol&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:44.280
for Ireland itself, and the subject of&nbsp;
one of its most famous tunes. The song,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:48.600
written in or around the 16th century,&nbsp;
has become a traditional Irish ballad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:52.860
and as such, has become an integral&nbsp;
part of the country's folk tradition.

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:57.660
Because "The Witcher: Blood Origin" introduces&nbsp;
the first Witcher to ever prowl the Continent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.660 --> 00:07:01.200
it naturally has to also introduce&nbsp;
the first Trial of the Grasses:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:06.240
the process by which Witchers are made. In one&nbsp;
fascinating Easter egg, it turns out that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:10.560
ingredients that went into this first beta&nbsp;
test of a Trial share a lot in common with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:16.860
the ingredient list detailed in "The Witcher&nbsp;
III" — set 1,200 years after this series. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:20.734
Zacaré and the rest of the team gather herbs&nbsp;
to begin the Trial, she claims to require,

00:07:20.878 --> 00:07:25.500
"There are Feainnewedd flowers in this cave,&nbsp;
but I'll need you to collect mandrake root,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:29.760
wolf's-bane, and corpse nettle and we'll need&nbsp;
the heart of the beast back at the monolith."

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:34.080
When Geralt and company try to recreate this&nbsp;
Decoction of the Grasses in "The Witcher III,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:39.120
the recipe is similar. Mandrake root&nbsp;
remains, as does the body part of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:43.440
monster. The major difference seems to be that&nbsp;
later Witchers find common plants sufficient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:48.580
substitutes for the much rarer wolf's-bane,&nbsp;
corpse nettle, and Feainnewedd flowers.

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:52.680
Though Avallac'h has relatively little screen&nbsp;
time and plot importance in "The Witcher:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:57.540
Blood Origin," one line of dialogue he just barely&nbsp;
manages to finish is one of the series's coolest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:02.280
Easter eggs. After he's allowed to pore over the&nbsp;
writings of the brilliant mage Syndril regarding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:06.900
interdimensional travel, Avallac'h excitedly&nbsp;
reports to Queen Merwyn that the Conjunctions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:11.220
might allow for time travel, as well as&nbsp;
interdimensional transit. The small detail&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:15.600
you might have missed here is that Avallac'h&nbsp;
is actually right — and Ciri has proven it.

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:20.040
In one of the more emotional scenes in&nbsp;
"The Witcher III," Geralt and Ciri share&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:24.360
a deep fireside talk, having just&nbsp;
reunited after years apart. As Ciri&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:28.740
recalls her pursuit by the Wild Hunt and the&nbsp;
protection of Avallac'h, she tells Geralt,

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:32.640
"He found a portal and took us to a&nbsp;
world where Eredin couldn't find us."

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:36.000
When Geralt asks her about&nbsp;
that world, Ciri recalls that

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:38.220
"People there had metal in their heads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:44.220
waged war from a distance using things similar&nbsp;
to megascopes. And there were no horses. "

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:48.960
Though Geralt doesn't believe her, Ciri is&nbsp;
telling the truth — she traveled into the future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:54.222
and as many fans believe, that future&nbsp;
is the setting of "Cyberpunk 2077."

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:59.160
One interesting phenomenon that appears twice&nbsp;
in "The Witcher: Blood Origin" is the concept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:04.620
of celestial twins. The first instance comes when&nbsp;
Balor tries to provide some unknown godlike deity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:09.840
with an appropriate sacrifice in exchange for&nbsp;
power. The mage offers the unseen force a pair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:14.400
of celestial siblings, thinking their cosmic&nbsp;
importance to be an adequate payment. Though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:18.900
he ends up being wrong, at least in the eyes of&nbsp;
the mysterious being, it's clear that celestial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:22.800
siblings are thought to hold an abundance&nbsp;
of magical power. When it's revealed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:27.540
Syndril and Zacaré are also celestial twins, and&nbsp;
that both their magical abilities are bolstered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:32.580
by that connection, Balor is confirmed to&nbsp;
be right. However, fans of the core series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.580 --> 00:09:37.200
"The Witcher," may already recognize celestial&nbsp;
siblings and the powerful destiny they share.

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:41.400
In the first episode of "The Witcher,"&nbsp;
Geralt is tasked with capturing Renfri,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:46.500
herself a celestial twin. The power behind&nbsp;
celestial twins lies in the date of their birth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:50.160
especially in the moon on the night&nbsp;
of their birth, and for Renfri,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:53.818
her birth came during a prophesied&nbsp;
eclipse known as the Black Sun.

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:58.860
It's a hard and fast rule within the Witcher&nbsp;
canon that women cannot become Witchers. According&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.860 --> 00:10:03.240
to the lore of the books, women are simply not&nbsp;
strong enough to endure the Trial of the Grasses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:07.260
let alone the myriad other challenges&nbsp;
that follow, and are therefore never even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:11.760
considered for the job. Luckily for gender&nbsp;
equality, and for common sense in general,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:15.720
"The Witcher: Blood Origin" has finally&nbsp;
taken a canon position to the contrary.

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:19.980
When Syndril and Zacaré suggest creating&nbsp;
a monster to fight Balor's creature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:24.120
they hint that only the strongest among them&nbsp;
has even the slightest hope of surviving the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:27.960
transformation. Based on that knowledge,&nbsp;
the group agrees that Éile, the Lark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:32.100
is the best candidate. That means that the first&nbsp;
Witcher to grace the continent is originally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:36.960
supposed to be female. It is only because Éile is&nbsp;
so beloved by Fjall, who can't bear the thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:42.060
of her dying or becoming a monster, that he&nbsp;
sneakily undergoes the trial in her place. So the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:47.038
first Witcher ends up male, and the ridiculous&nbsp;
idea that women can't be Witchers takes hold.

